    "q-213084": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213084",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "$-69\\text{ mod }6=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The residue of $-69$ modulo $6$ is the integer $0\\le r<6$ such that\n\n$$\n-69≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-69$ as an integer multiple of $6,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-69≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ because\n\n$$\n-69=(-12)\\cdot 6+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-69$ modulo $6$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-69 & ≡-69+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡-69+12\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡-69+72 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-69$ modulo $6$ is $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
